Based on my extensive research across 20+ searches covering SOTA verification, reference checking, competing work analysis, and feasibility assessment, here is my adversarial review:

```json
{
  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The strategy-level expert specialization (induction vs contradiction vs construction) is a genuine unclaimed gap — no existing work trains per-strategy LoRA experts with verifier routing. However, LongCat-Flash-Prover (Mar 2026, 560B MoE) already trains task-level specialized experts (auto-formalization, sketching, proving) with verifier feedback, making this a moderate incremental step from task-level to strategy-level specialization rather than a conceptual breakthrough. The idea combines known ingredients (LoRA MoE, GRPO, α-divergence, Lean verification) in a new configuration but the question of whether strategy-level diversity actually matters more than token-level or task-level diversity is unaddressed."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The weakest link is strategy classification of 220K Mathlib proofs into 5-6 clean families. Real proofs often mix strategies (induction with case splits, contradiction leading to construction). The noise from impure labels could undermine expert specialization entirely. The AAAI 2027 deadline (Jul 28) is only 9 weeks away — incompatible with the 14-18 week manual timeline or even the 10-14 week human-in-loop estimate. The 'verifier as perfect arbiter' claim is overstated: fraction-of-have-lemmas-type-checking is a weak proxy for strategy quality."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The proposal is well-organized with a thorough landscape analysis. All 13 references verified as real. The approach pipeline (data extraction → expert SFT → router training → RL → evaluation) is clearly specified. Strategy families and metrics are concretely defined."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 2,
      "rationale": "The claimed 3-8% pass@32 improvement over Goedel-Prover-V2-8B (84.6%) would yield ~87-92%, far below current SOTA: LongCat-Flash-Prover reaches 97.1% on miniF2F, Seed-Prover 1.5 'saturates' it. miniF2F is effectively solved for large models. An 8B-scale method cannot compete with 560B-671B SOTA systems. The strategy-entropy metric is interesting but the practical impact of strategy diversity on harder benchmarks (PutnamBench) is speculative and unvalidated."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "CRITICAL: miniF2F is approaching saturation (97%+ with LongCat-Flash-Prover, Seed-Prover 1.5 saturates it). A 3-8% improvement over GP-V2-8B on a nearly-solved benchmark provides minimal evidence of the approach's value.",
    "CRITICAL: LongCat-Flash-Prover (Mar 2026) — a 560B MoE with task-specialized experts and verifier feedback achieving 97.1% miniF2F and 41.5% PutnamBench — is not discussed. This is the most relevant competing work and significantly narrows the novelty gap.",
    "Strategy classification feasibility is unvalidated. Mathlib proofs frequently combine multiple strategies (induction + case analysis, contradiction + algebraic rewriting). The assumption of clean strategy families is questionable.",
    "AAAI 2027 timeline is infeasible: 9 weeks to deadline vs 14-18 week (manual) or 10-14 week (human-in-loop) estimate.",
    "ICLR 2027 dates (Sep 19/24) are unverified — ICLR 2027 CFP has not been officially announced as of May 2026.",
    "The 'verifier as perfect arbiter' framing overstates what Lean4 provides. Lean checks type-correctness of individual tactics, not strategy quality. The fraction of have-lemmas that type-check is a noisy proxy.",
    "Scale mismatch: proposing an 8B model when competitive SOTA systems (DeepSeek-Prover-V2-671B, LongCat-Flash-Prover-560B, Aristotle-200B+) are 25-80x larger.",
    "The 'Decoupled Reasoning and Proving' (arXiv 2507.06804) paper, which uses specialized Reasoner+Prover models with strategic lemma generation, is not cited despite being closely related.",
    "Strategy faithfulness reward ('structural match between decomposition plan and final proof') is underspecified — no concrete metric definition is provided."
  ],
  "feedback": "This idea has a genuine novelty kernel (strategy-family-specialized experts) but needs significant revisions: (1) Drop miniF2F as the primary benchmark — it's nearly saturated. Focus on PutnamBench and ProofNet where ceiling effects are absent and strategic diversity should matter more. (2) Add LongCat-Flash-Prover and 'Decoupled Reasoning and Proving' to the landscape analysis; explicitly argue why strategy-level specialization beats task-level specialization. (3) Provide preliminary evidence that clean strategy classification is feasible — e.g., run a small pilot on 1000 Mathlib proofs showing inter-annotator agreement or cluster separation. (4) Drop AAAI 2027 as target venue (infeasible timeline); target ICLR 2027 or NeurIPS 2027. (5) Reconsider the improvement target — 3-8% over an already-surpassed baseline is uncompelling. Instead, emphasize the diversity angle: show that strategy-diverse proofs cover problems that monolithic provers miss, especially on hard competition benchmarks. (6) Define the strategy faithfulness reward concretely. (7) Tone down the 'perfect arbiter' claim — acknowledge the limitations of subgoal type-checking as a strategy-quality signal.",
  "reason": "Genuine novelty in strategy-level expert specialization, but outdated baselines (miniF2F nearly saturated), missing critical competitor (LongCat-Flash-Prover), unvalidated strategy classification, and infeasible AAAI timeline require substantial revision."
}
```
